igma_race": 7.5, "sigma_total": 8.18, "baseline_margin": -8.53, "analyst_adjustment": -1.47, "key_factors": ["Stauber's dominant fundraising: $2.0M raised and $1.2M on hand versus Swanson's $317K raised and $90K banked", "R+7 district lean and an incumbent who has won the seat comfortably in prior cycles", "Unanimous expert ratings of Likely-to-Safe R; markets price Swanson near 14%", "No public general-election polling; a pro-Democratic midterm environment is the main unknown"], "rationale": "Stauber combines incumbency, a seven-point Republican district lean, and a lopsided money advantage against a first-time challenger, and every major rater places the seat at Likely R or better while markets give Swanson roughly a one-in-seven chance. A favorable midterm climate for Democrats keeps this short of Safe R, but nothing in the evidence suggests a genuinely competitive race.", "watch": "Independent polling showing Swanson competitive on the Iron Range, or a decisive national Democratic wave, would be the clearest sign the race is tightening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Pete Stauber (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Pete Stauber, the Republican incumbent, seeks another term in a northeastern Minnesota district that leans Republican by about seven points and that he has carried comfortably in previous runs. His challenger, Democrat Trina Swanson, won the DFL endorsement and her primary, but enters the general election with far less money: roughly $317,000 raised and $90,000 on hand, against Stauber's $2.0 million raised and $1.2 million banked.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals and outside assessments. Rating outlets uniformly place the seat in Republican-favoring categories ranging from Likely to Safe R, and prediction markets give Swanson roughly a one-in-seven chance of winning. A midterm environment tilted toward Democrats is the main force working in her favor, but the district's lean, Stauber's incumbency, and the financial gap all point toward a Republican hold.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0911, "margin": -8.65}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1026, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1108, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1084, "margin": -10.0}]}